into groups where we would much prefer people

00:02:05.999 --> 00:02:11.760
rather than having to act out of allegiance
to party, act out of understanding the policy

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:13.180
positions of an individual.

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:14.470
So I love the idea.

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:16.710
The problem is the following.

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:21.350
A lot of people, uh, are not going to actually
do the work of figuring out what are the policy

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:23.630
positions of individuals.

00:02:23.630 --> 00:02:31.330
And you will open up to a whole new type of
attack ad and campaign ad which can say even

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:34.990
crazier things about the positions of a candidate.

00:02:34.990 --> 00:02:40.990
And without any sorting method whatsoever,
you may even end up with a less informed electorate.

00:02:40.990 --> 00:02:42.450
If you can imagine that.

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:45.910
Like it's bad enough when people show up and
say, I'm just going to vote for every Democrat.

00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:49.150
I'm just going to vote for every Republican
with without regard for what it is that they

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:50.490
stand for.

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:55.450
But in our low information environment to
some degree, it provides at least an initial

00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:58.500
sorting method that maybe is useful.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:01.320
So it's, it's a bad in all ways.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:02.370
I love the idea.

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:06.040
I think at the national level it could have
unintended consequences that we don't like,

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:10.590
but I'm glad you're thinking about it because
D the two party do.

